                <pb facs="d72-02" n="2" xml:id="d72-02"/>


                <head>Note From Richard Kennedy of the National Security Council Staff to the
                    President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs (Kissinger)</head>
                <dateline><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date when="1971-03-19">March 19,
                        1971</date></dateline>
                <p>HAK:<lb/>I sympathize with Hal’s view but (1) the F–5’s can’t do any significant
                    harm in the Arab-Israeli contest, (2) if we don’t sell them the French or the
                    Russians will and (3) there may be a way we could use them to loosen up the aid
                    talks (and here we have a real economic interest).</p>
                <p>Obviously there would be domestic criticism unless a good aid settlement
                    emerges.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>RTK</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
